📘 Laptop Battery Health – Quick Summary

1. Battery Types

  - Li-Ion: Liquid electrolyte, cylindrical shape
  - Li-Po: Gel/solid electrolyte, flexible shape
  - Shape differs, battery life is similar.

2. Signs of Battery Degradation

  - Shorter battery life
  - Overheating
  - Swelling (may cause cracks)
  - Sudden shutdowns (even when charged)
  - Won't hold a charge

3. Check Battery Health

  - OS	Command/Tool
  - Windows	powercfg /batteryreport (HTML file)
  - Mac	About This Mac → System Report → Power
  - Third-party	BatteryInfoView (Win), coconutBattery (Mac)

4. Factors Draining Battery

  - High brightness
  - Background processes
  - Full charge/discharge cycles (0% → 100%)
  - Heat exposure
  - High performance settings

5. Best Practices for Longer Battery Life

  - Keep charge between 20–80% (never fully drain)
  - Lower screen brightness
  - Close unused apps/processes
  - Use power-saving modes
  - Store in cool, dry place
  - Avoid full discharges (0%)
  - Avoid constant 100% charging

6. When to Replace Battery

  - Health below 50% capacity
  - Cycle count exceeds manufacturer limit
  - Sudden shutdowns or swelling
  - Won't hold a charge at all

7. Replacement Steps

  - Verify correct battery model
  - Power off and unplug device
  - External: Use slide-release lock
  - Internal: Open case to access battery
  - Remove old, insert new, secure

8. Post-Replacement Calibration

  - Only time to fully charge and discharge!
  - Charge to 100%
  - Discharge to 5–10%
  - Recharge to 100%
  - Why: Helps system track battery status accurately
